NXR-113|Static device registry|Drivers|The kernel shall register permanent device objects before scheduling and seal a finite registry against runtime registration.|Stable lifetime without heap.|Duplicate identities and exclusive MMIO overlaps fail; unavailable devices cannot open; register-only drivers need no worker.|Future boot-registry boundary review and tests.|NXR-001,NXR-002|RTOS_DEVICE proposed, off in nano.|Fixed object table and per-device state; zero runtime allocation.|include/rtos.hpp,docs/drivers.rst,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 001-005 require a sealed device registry; the current thread registry is not a device registry.
NXR-114|Minimal driver operations|Drivers|The driver framework shall define initialize, read, write and typed control operations with explicit context, ownership and result contracts.|Make small FPGA drivers easy to implement.|Missing optional operations return unsupported without MMIO; example register driver needs no queue, heap or hidden thread; callback blocking and bounded-work rules are documented.|Future API review and example-driver checks.|NXR-113|Optional direct-access driver module.|One permanent object and operations table per device; target size reported.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 006-010 define a proposed operations interface; existing Ethernet callbacks do not provide this general framework.
NXR-115|Whole device transaction lock|Synchronization|The driver framework shall serialize each complete logical device transaction across all thread entry points using the same priority-inheriting mutex.|Prevent interleaved command/data/status sequences.|Concurrent callers cannot interleave transaction steps; normal errors release ownership; aliases follow the same lock; reserved hardware stays owned across completion waits.|Future forced-preemption transaction scenarios.|NXR-114,NXR-116|Direct locked access default when device sharing is enabled.|One static mutex per serialization domain; no mandatory request queue.|include/rtos.hpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 011-015 require transaction scope rather than per-register locking; mutex wait state is not currently implemented.
NXR-116|Bounded priority inheritance|Synchronization|The kernel shall implement owner-aware nonrecursive mutexes with separate base and effective priorities and bounded transitive priority donation.|Bound priority inversion under fixed-priority scheduling.|Donation propagates through valid chains; timeout, unlock and priority change recompute donations; highest effective priority then FIFO selects waiters; self-lock and cycles fail before sleep.|Future inversion, chained donation and cycle scenarios.|NXR-001|RTOS_MUTEX_PI proposed; dependency required by shared-device locking.|Static mutex objects and thread wait metadata; traversal bounded by thread count.|include/rtos.hpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 016-020 identify missing separate effective priority and lock ownership in the current public Thread.
NXR-117|Typed MMIO access contracts|Drivers|The port and driver framework shall validate register aperture, width and alignment and define ordering, endian and side-effect semantics for MMIO.|Avoid invalid or destructive register operations.|Overflowing or out-of-aperture accesses fail before hardware access; read-only and write-one-to-clear registers use appropriate operations; barriers are port-defined; no implicit destructive readback.|Future MMIO fake-backend review and target ordering qualification.|NXR-114|Per-device register map and per-port ordering hooks.|Compile-time register descriptors plus bounded validation; no heap.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 021-025 distinguish typed volatile register access from verified hardware ordering and register semantics.
NXR-118|Race-free completion wait|Synchronization|The kernel shall atomically enroll device waiters with condition checks and use persistent generation-tagged completion state and one absolute deadline.|Prevent missed interrupts and stale completion reuse.|Completion before or during wait enrollment is preserved; wake rechecks predicate; old generations cannot complete new commands; retries do not extend deadline.|Future interrupt-at-each-boundary and timeout-race scenarios.|NXR-115,NXR-128|Per-device completion predicate and finite deadline.|Static completion generation, predicate and waiter metadata.|src/scheduler.hpp,docs/drivers.rst,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 026-030 require a new wait protocol; existing relative thread sleep is insufficient for device completion.
NXR-119|Explicit ISR operation boundary|Interrupts|The kernel shall provide separately named nonblocking ISR-safe operations with bounded work and deferred rescheduling at interrupt exit.|Keep peripheral interrupts responsive and context-safe.|Blocking APIs reject interrupt context; BSP documents acknowledge and nesting; higher-priority unblocked thread runs at the permitted boundary; peripheral IRQ capability remains unavailable until dispatch exists.|Future external-interrupt dispatch and nesting qualification.|NXR-118|Per-port external interrupt support explicitly enabled only when qualified.|Static handlers and bounded deferred state; no ISR heap.|docs/drivers.rst,include/rtos.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 031-035 preserve the current timer-only interrupt limitation and do not claim an implemented ISR IPC API.
NXR-120|AXI timeout and fault isolation|Drivers|The driver framework shall distinguish lock timeout, device completion timeout and AXI access failure and keep uncertain hardware faulted until owned recovery establishes quiescence.|Software cannot recover every stalled hardware bus access.|FPGA integration specifies bounded bus error response or independent timeout; partial commands fault device; reset requires ownership and IRQ quiescence; no timeout is treated as proof that hardware stopped.|Future stalled-device and interconnect fault qualification.|NXR-117,NXR-118|Board-specified AXI error responder, timeout and reset policy.|Static fault state and counters; hardware timeout resource declared separately.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 036-040 require hardware-level progress support; thread deadlines alone cannot bound a CPU-stalling MMIO load.
NXR-121|Optional queued device service|Drivers|The driver framework shall optionally serialize slow operations through a statically provisioned request queue and worker with explicit data lifetime and cancellation rules.|Support slow devices without penalizing simple register drivers.|Queue and worker are opt-in; capacity exhaustion returns full; ordering policy is documented; retained data stays valid; queued cancellation differs from cancellation after issuing hardware commands; DMA remains deferred.|Future worker ownership and cancellation scenarios.|NXR-114,NXR-122,NXR-128|Optional worker, capacity and FIFO or priority policy.|Fixed request storage plus explicit worker control block and stack.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 041-045 define an optional service model with no mandatory per-device thread and no DMA support.
NXR-122|Typed static message queue|IPC|The kernel shall provide fixed-capacity Queue<T,N> objects for positive N and initially trivially copyable messages with copied ownership and FIFO message order.|Simple allocation-free communication between threads.|Successful send copies payload before return; receive preserves message order; no constructors or allocator calls hide in transfer; target payload, alignment, control and waiter costs are reported.|Future queue ownership, boundary and footprint scenarios.|NXR-002|RTOS_QUEUE proposed; capacity and message type fixed at boot.|N times sizeof(T) plus exact control/alignment and static wait metadata.|include/rtos_static.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 046-050 explicitly distinguish the existing unsynchronized Ring from the proposed scheduler-integrated queue.
NXR-123|Timed queue transfer semantics|IPC|The kernel shall provide immediate and timed queue send and receive with priority-ordered waiters, FIFO ties and exactly one transfer-or-timeout outcome.|Predictable blocking with no stolen grants.|NoWait returns full or empty immediately; receive failure preserves output; transfer and timeout serialize; selected waiter owns the granted slot or message before competitors can steal it.|Future queue full, empty, handoff and simultaneous-timeout scenarios.|NXR-122,NXR-128|NoWait, bounded timeout and documented indefinite-wait option.|Wait state lives in permanent thread metadata; no per-wait allocation.|src/scheduler.hpp,include/rtos_static.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 051-055 define queue blocking behavior absent from the current scheduler state model.
NXR-124|ISR-safe queue transfer|IPC|The kernel shall provide bounded nonblocking queue operations for supported interrupt contexts using a verified port synchronization protocol.|Allow IRQ completion messages without sleeping.|ISR send and receive return full or empty explicitly; nested producers preserve indices and ownership; no overwrite or unbounded retry; configurable payload cap bounds ISR copy work.|Future nested-interrupt queue and work-bound qualification.|NXR-119,NXR-122|Explicit ISR transfer methods and maximum ISR message bytes.|Same fixed queue storage plus port critical-section state.|include/rtos_static.hpp,docs/drivers.rst,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 056-060 reject using the current single-owner Ring directly from concurrent threads and interrupts.
NXR-125|Event flag wait semantics|IPC|The kernel shall provide fixed-width event flags with validated masks, wait-any, wait-all, returned observed bits and atomic optional clear-on-success.|Express persistent readiness conditions without payload queues.|Zero and unsupported masks fail unchanged; timeout consumes no bits; repeated sets coalesce; wait-all requires all requested bits; counting use cases are directed to semaphores.|Future mask, any/all and clear-on-success scenarios.|NXR-128|RTOS_EVENTS proposed; documented usable mask and clear mode.|One static flag word and bounded waiter metadata per event object.|include/rtos.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 061-065 define event conditions rather than counted signals; no event primitive is currently implemented.
NXR-126|Event wake and payload ordering|IPC|The kernel shall serialize event updates and wait enrollment and define priority consumption and broadcast behavior separately.|Prevent lost updates and ambiguous multi-waiter consumption.|Non-clearing waits evaluate a documented snapshot; consuming waits evaluate by priority and remaining bits; ISR set never blocks; payload ownership and visibility require an explicit separate contract.|Future multi-waiter clear, broadcast and ISR-set scenarios.|NXR-125,NXR-119|Explicit broadcast versus consuming wait policy.|Fixed event state and existing thread wait metadata.|src/scheduler.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 066-070 resolve multi-waiter semantics and avoid implying that event flags transfer payload ownership.
NXR-127|Bounded counting semaphore|IPC|The kernel shall provide statically allocated counting semaphores with validated initial and maximum counts, bounded waits and direct priority waiter handoff.|Count work or resource availability without ownership confusion.|Overflow fails unchanged; release grants selected waiter before exposing count to contenders; timeout cannot duplicate tokens; ISR release is nonblocking; semaphore has no ownership or priority inheritance.|Future count conservation and release-timeout race scenarios.|NXR-128,NXR-119|RTOS_SEMAPHORE proposed; boot-fixed initial and maximum counts.|Fixed count, maximum and waiter metadata; zero heap.|include/rtos.hpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 071-075 separate counted notifications from owner-aware mutexes and require token conservation.
NXR-128|Bounded thread wait lifecycle|Synchronization|The kernel shall track at most one active blocking operation per thread initially and remove wait membership exactly once on grant, timeout or supported termination.|Keep scheduler and IPC object state consistent.|Blocking before start or from idle fails; timeout range respects modular tick limits; stale wake cannot affect a later wait; internal links are not application-owned; each path removes one wait registration.|Future wait-state transition and stale-wakeup scenarios.|NXR-001|One active wait per thread; supported finite tick range documented.|Permanent thread wait metadata with bounded object lookup.|include/rtos.hpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 076-080 identify required scheduler changes; current State only represents unused, ready, sleeping and finished.
NXR-129|Abandoned device ownership|Synchronization|The kernel shall report owner-dead and fault a device when its mutex owner exits during an uncertain transaction until explicit recovery restores a safe state.|Avoid exposing partially programmed hardware as ready.|Waiters receive distinct failure; no implicit successful unlock; donation and wait references are removed; recovery requires ownership; fatal corruption is not promised recoverable.|Future owner-exit at each transaction boundary scenarios.|NXR-115,NXR-116,NXR-120,NXR-128|Explicit owner-dead recovery policy; no dynamic thread reclamation.|Static abandonment and recovery state; no reallocation.|include/rtos.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 081-085 reject silently releasing hardware ownership on thread exit without device-state recovery.
NXR-130|Device and IPC metrics|Diagnostics|The kernel shall copy coherent bounded device and IPC snapshots into caller-provided storage with separate contention, capacity, timeout and fault statistics.|Expose contention and resource sizing to applications.|Snapshots include mutex owner and effective priority, waiter counts, queue capacity/depth/high-water, full results, timeout/error/recovery; counters saturate safely; count-only query allocates nothing.|Future snapshot coherence and saturation scenarios.|NXR-116,NXR-122,NXR-128|Metrics independently configurable; costs reported when enabled.|Fixed per-object counters and caller-owned output arrays.|include/rtos.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 086-090 extend existing thread snapshot principles without claiming implemented device or IPC metrics.
NXR-131|Modular synchronization configuration|Configuration|The build configuration shall independently enable device, queue, event, semaphore and mutex modules with validated dependencies and explicit single-hart support limits.|Preserve the minimal footprint and a future multiprocessor seam.|Nano disables all new modules; invalid dependencies fail configuration; disabled storage disappears; IRQ masking is never advertised as an SMP lock; CMake and C# XDoc policies remain in force.|Future configuration matrix and target footprint audits.|NXR-002|Proposed RTOS_DEVICE, RTOS_QUEUE, RTOS_EVENTS, RTOS_SEMAPHORE and RTOS_MUTEX_PI.|Disabled features contribute zero module storage; enabled sizes measured per target.|docs/drivers.rst,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 091-095 specify future switches and portability boundaries rather than claiming existing configurable IPC modules.
NXR-132|Small bounded logging and documentation|Diagnostics|The kernel shall keep optional static logging capacity configurable, use drop-new initially, bound synchronous drain work and document all proposed APIs and deferred DMA scope honestly.|Avoid a mandatory 31 KiB log queue and ambiguous lifetime claims.|Depth and payload have explicit costs; truncation and full losses differ; buffers remain owned until consumed; ISR formatting is context-safe; no unverified RAM saving or DMA cancellation claim appears in the manual.|Future logging capacity, drain budget and ownership scenarios.|NXR-002,NXR-131|Logging off in nano; explicit fixed depth and message bytes.|Depth times actual slot size plus formatting and synchronization metadata reported separately.|include/rtos_static.hpp,docs/drivers.rst,docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Reviews 096-100 preserve a small optional logger and explicitly label the 100 decisions as design review without build or runtime proof.

